      "Abstract": {
        "CopyrightInformation": "© 2014 John Wiley & Sons A/S. Published by John Wiley & Sons Ltd.",
        "AbstractText": [
          {
            "NlmCategory": "BACKGROUND",
            "Label": "BACKGROUND",
            "content": "Early signs of renal complications can be common in youths with type 1 diabetes (T1D). Recently, there has been an increasing interest in potential renal complications associated with obesity, paralleling the epidemics of this condition, although there are limited data in children."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "OBJECTIVE",
            "Label": "HYPOTHESIS",
            "content": "Obese children and adolescents present signs of early alterations in renal function similar to non-obese peers with T1D."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
            "Label": "SUBJECTS",
            "content": "Eighty-three obese (age: 11.6\u2009±\u20093.0\u2009yr), 164 non-obese T1D (age: 12.4\u2009±\u20093.2\u2009yr), and 71 non-obese control (age: 12.3\u2009±\u20093.2\u2009yr) children and adolescents were enrolled in the study."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
            "Label": "METHODS",
            "content": "Anthropometric parameters and blood pressure were measured. Renal function was assessed by albumin excretion rate (AER), serum cystatin C, creatinine and estimated glomerular filtration rate (e-GFR), calculated using the Bouvet's formula."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
            "Label": "RESULTS",
            "content": "Obese and non-obese T1D youths had similar AER [8.9(5.9-10.8) vs. 8.7(5.9-13.1) µg/min] and e-GFR levels (114.8\u2009±\u200919.6 vs. 113.4\u2009±\u200919.1\u2009mL/min), which were higher than in controls [AER: 8.1(5.9-8.7) µg/min, e-GFR: 104.7\u2009±\u200918.9\u2009mL/min]. Prevalence of microalbuminuria and hyperfiltration was similar between obese and T1D youths and higher than their control peers (6.0 vs. 8.0 vs. 0%, p\u2009=\u20090.02; 15.9 vs. 15.9 vs. 4.3%, p\u2009=\u20090.03, respectively). Body mass index (BMI) z-score was independently related to e-GFR (r\u2009=\u20090.328; p\u2009<\u20090.001), and AER (r\u2009=\u20090.138; p\u2009=\u20090.017). Hemoglobin A1c (HbA1c) correlated with AER (r\u2009=\u20090.148; p\u2009=\u20090.007) but not with eGFR (r\u2009=\u20090.041; p\u2009=\u20090.310)."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
            "Label": "CONCLUSIONS",
            "content": "Obese children and adolescents show early alterations in renal function, compared to normal weight peers, and they have similar renal profiles than age-matched peers with T1D."
          }
        ]
      },
